===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|traffick|traffique|q2=obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|frm|trafique}}, {{m|frm|traffique||traffic}}, from {{der|en|it|traffico||traffic}} from {{m|it|trafficare||to carry on trade}}. Potentially from {{der|en|VL.|*trānsfrīcāre||to rub across}}; Klein instead suggests the Italian has ultimate origin in {{der|en|ar|تَفْرِيق||distribution, dispersion}}, reshaped to match the native prefix {{m|it|tra-||trans-}}.
{{root|en|ar|ف ر ق}}<!--possibly-->

The adjective sense is possibly influenced by {{der|en|tl|trapik}} and follows a general trend in Philippine English to make nouns adjectives.<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=traffic|pos=adj|id=33840333|date=December 2021}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|trăf'ĭk}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹæfɪk/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-traffic.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æfɪk|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# Moving [[pedestrian]]s or [[vehicle]]s, or the [[flux]] or [[passage]] thereof.
#: {{ux|en|The '''traffic''' is slow during rush hour.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet Q1-2|chapter=Prologue|passage=VVhoſe miſaduentures, piteous ouerthrovves, / (Through the continuing of their Fathers ſtrife, / And death-markt paſſage of their Parents rage) / Is novv the tvvo hovvres '''traffique''' of our Stage.}}
# [[commercial|Commercial]] [[transportation]] or [[exchange]] of [[goods]], or the movement of [[passenger]]s or people.
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|passage=I had three large axes, and abundance of hatchets (for we carried the hatchets for '''traffic''' with the Indians).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910|chapter={{w|The Traffic in Women}}|title={{w|Anarchism and Other Essays}}|author=Emma Goldman|authorlink=Emma Goldman|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2162/pg2162-images.html|passage=To assume that the recent investigation of the white slave '''traffic''' (and, by the way, a very superficial investigation) has discovered anything new, is, to say the least, very foolish}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=John Darwin|title=After Tamerlane|publisher=Penguin|page=12
|passage=Its units of study are regions or oceans, long-distance trades [...], the '''traffic''' of cults and beliefs between cultures and continents.}}
# [[illegal|Illegal]] trade or exchange of goods, often [[drug]]s.
#: {{syn|en|trafficking<q:more common>}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Alfred W. McCoy|title=How the heroin trade explains the US-UK failure in Afghanistan|work=The Guardian|date=2018-01-09|url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/2018/jan/09/how-the-heroin-trade-explains-the-us-uk-failure-in-afghanistan|issn=0261-3077|passage=They, in turn, had long dominated the drug '''traffic''' in the area of north-east Afghanistan that they controlled during the Taliban years.}}
# Exchange or [[flux]] of [[information]], [[message]]s or [[data]], as in a computer or telephone [[network]].
#* '''1902''', John Buchan, ''The Outgoing of the Tide''
#*: The parish stank of idolatry, abominable rites were practiced in secret, and in all the bounds there was no one had a more evil name for the black '''traffic''' than one Alison Sempill, who bode at the Skerburnfoot.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Charles Arthur|title=Porn sites get more internet traffic in UK than social networks or shopping|work=The Guardian|date=2013-07-26|url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2013/jul/26/porn-sites-internet-traffic-uk|issn=0261-3077|passage=Internet '''traffic''' to legal pornography sites in the UK comprised 8.5% of all "clicks" on web pages in June – exceeding those for shopping, news, business or social networks, according to new data obtained exclusively by the Guardian.}}
## {{lb|en|radio}} In [[CB radio]], formal written messages relayed on behalf of others.
## {{lb|en|advertising}} The amount of attention paid to a particular printed page etc. in a publication.
##* '''1950''', ''Advertising & Selling'' (volume 43, part 2, page 53)
##*: Those fixed locations which are sold to advertisers become preferred according to the expected '''page traffic'''.
# Commodities of the market.
#* {{quote-book|en|1716|{{w|John Gay}}|{{w|Trivia (poem)|Trivia, or The Art of Walking the Streets of London}}|https://rpo.library.utoronto.ca/poems/trivia-or-art-walking-streets-london}}
#*: You'll see a draggled damsel / From Billingsgate her fishy '''traffic''' bear.

